### run 4
 morphology -- erode / dilate / morphologyEx vs OpenCV
================================================================================

OpenCV 4.8.0-dev, cv::getNumThreads() = 1; binCV is single-threaded

DENOMINATOR: cv::erode / cv::dilate / cv::morphologyEx on
the SAME binary content stored as CV_8U, with the same structuring element,
anchor and border. That is what a user does today without binCV.

binCV rows: ops/morphology.hpp at uint32 (the default word type,) and
uint64. erode and dilate use NO scratch; morphologyEx(OPEN) uses exactly one
caller-provided frame.

Working set is one call's live buffers, not a per-buffer ratio (CLAUDE.md,
peak). Both columns are ratios against the OpenCV row.

FIXED PER-CALL COST (hazard 5) is measured on a 2x2 frame FOR EACH CASE and
printed on that case's PER-FRAME line -- it is the part of the row that is not
the operation. Divided by a frame's pixel count it is what a small frame's
ns/pixel is mostly made of. It is NOT subtracted from the tables.

